WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:01.410
JENNY BROWN: Let's face it.

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.240
We've all got control issues.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.550
They might keep us up
at night, and sometimes

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.770
they even limit our ability
to work with others.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:12.330
Fortunately, Cloud Storage
Bytes is here to help.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:15.690
Well, as long as your
particular control issues

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.820
revolve around dealing
with and managing

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.830
versioning in the Cloud.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.644
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.800
Have you ever accidentally
deleted a file

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.360
that you meant to keep?

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.640
What about if someone
else deletes your stuff?

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.130
It's frustrating,
to say the least.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:37.060
More importantly, imagine this
experience for your users.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:39.460
Fortunately, you can
course correct and monitor

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.580
potential accidental data
removal from your Cloud Storage

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.280
bucket thanks to
object versioning.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.830
For a quick review,
Google Cloud Storage

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:50.530
uses object versioning to
protect your Cloud Storage

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:53.890
data from being overwritten
or accidentally deleted.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.510
Sounds great, right?

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:58.630
So let's get right into how
this versioning thing works.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.940
Object versioning gets
enabled on a bucket

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.090
using gsutil or the REST API.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:07.480
Once enabled, Cloud Storage
creates an archived version

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.600
of an object each time the
live version of the object

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.060
is overwritten or deleted.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.340
That being said,
if you move a file,

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.170
it effectively
loses its history.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.940
So for example, if I have
file cat with three versions

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.560
and I move cat to chonker,
then cat is still there

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.720
with three versions,
and now chonker

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.000
is there with one version.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.060
But as far as Cloud
Storage can tell,

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.810
there is no connection showing
that chonker was once cat.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:40.180
As for overwritten
or deleted objects,

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.180
the archived version keeps
the name of the object,

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.570
but is uniquely identified
by a generation number.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:49.870
All objects have generation
numbers associated with them,

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:52.960
but only archived objects
require generation numbers

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.880
to identify them.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.160
Whenever object
versioning is enabled,

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.920
you can list archived
versions of an object,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.700
restore the live version of
an object to an older state,

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:06.190
or permanently delete
an archived version

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:10.030
as needed using gsutil
or the REST API.

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:14.380
You can turn versioning on or
off for a bucket at any time.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.930
Turning off versioning
leaves existing object

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.750
versions in place,
but going forward,

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:24.040
the bucket stops accumulating
new archived object versions.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.290
Cloud Storage uses two
different properties

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:29.740
that, together, identify
the version of an object.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:33.250
One property identifies the
version of the object's data.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.350
The other property
identifies the version

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.360
of the object's metadata.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.450
These properties are always
present with every version

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:45.100
of the object, even if object
versioning is not enabled.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.720
They can be used
as preconditions

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.730
for conditional
updates to enforce

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.920
the ordering of those updates.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.570
Archived objects have
their own metadata,

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.860
which may differ from the
metadata of live objects.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.740
Most importantly here
is an archived version

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.180
retains its access
control lists,

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.990
so it may not necessarily
have the same permissions

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:08.110
as the live version
of that same object.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:10.510
Each object, whether
live or versioned,

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.220
has one set of metadata.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.300
Only the latest metageneration
numbers refer to that metadata.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.390
Older metageneration numbers
cannot be used to access

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.940
metadata that has
since been changed.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.580
You can update metadata
for an archived object

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:27.790
by specifying its
generation in your request.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:31.040
To ensure safe
read-modify-write semantics,

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.710
you can use a
metageneration-match

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:34.150
precondition.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.340
Using this precondition
causes the update

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.010
to fail if the metadata
you're attempting to update

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:42.580
was changed between the time you
read the metadata and the time

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:43.720
you sent the update.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.720
Now, all of this sounds
pretty abstract, so let's

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.250
explore an example.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.980
Let's see what happens to
the file cat in a bucket

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:55.090
with object versioning enabled
as you overwrite, update, move,

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.920
and delete the file.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.890
You start by uploading
an image called cat.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:02.260
When you first upload
cat to Cloud Storage,

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:06.020
it receives a generation number
and a metageneration number.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:09.670
In this example, the
generation number is this.

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:13.830
Because the object is new, the
metageneration number is 1.

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:16.870
Cat receives generation
and metageneration numbers

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:20.570
even though object
versioning is not enabled.

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:22.910
For instructions,
see the documentation

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:26.360
for viewing the object
metadata linked below.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:29.960
Now, you decide to enable object
versioning for your bucket.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.600
This doesn't affect the
generation or metageneration

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.930
numbers of cat.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:37.670
But what about when you
update the metadata?

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.880
Say you update the
metadata for cat

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.130
by adding a custom metadata--

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:44.590
color, black.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:47.770
Updating metadata causes the
metageneration value of cat

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:51.460
to increase, in this
case, from 1 to 2.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:55.490
However, the object
itself remains unchanged.

00:04:55.490 --> 00:05:00.220
So Cloud Storage continues to
store only one version of cat,

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:04.760
and the version still has
the same generation number.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.800
Now you upload a
new version of cat

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.200
to your Cloud Storage bucket.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.030
When you do so,
object versioning

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:15.680
moves the existing cat object
into an archived state.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.730
The archived version retains the
same storage class and metadata

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:21.650
it previously had.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.840
The archived version
appears only if you

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:25.970
perform a versioned listing.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:29.210
It does not appear in the
normal listing commands.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:33.930
The archived version is
now referenced like this.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.020
Meanwhile, the
newly uploaded cat

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.820
becomes the live
version of the object.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:43.000
This new cat gets its
own generation number.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:46.270
It also gets its own metadata
and a metageneration number

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:48.970
of 1, which means
it does not contain

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:54.090
the color black metadata
unless you specify it.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.610
When you access or
modify cat, this

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.830
is the version that's used.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:01.890
You can alternatively refer
to this version of cat

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:04.180
using its generation number.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:07.080
For example, when
using the gsutil tool,

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:10.920
you would refer to it
with this file name.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.500
What if I decide to
move cat to chonker?

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:16.750
Now when I view the
archived versions,

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:20.080
I find that cat is
now fully archived.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:21.940
The metageneration
number for chonker

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:26.340
is 1 even though there
were two versions of cat.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:28.770
When moving an object
in Cloud Storage,

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.380
you're actually making a
copy, and then deleting

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.970
the old version.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.910
This new object doesn't
retain the versioning data

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.800
of the old one.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:38.640
OK.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.730
You've decided you prefer
dogs, so you delete chonker.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:46.200
When you do this, the version
that had this generation number

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.360
becomes archived.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.970
Your bucket now contains
two archived version

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:54.090
of cat and no live versions.

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.550
You can still refer to
either archived version

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.980
by using its generation
number, but if you

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:04.090
try to access cat without a
generation number, it fails.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.880
Similarly, a normal object
listing of the bucket

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:10.360
will not show cat as one of
the objects in the bucket.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:13.930
For information on listing
archived versions of objects,

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:17.890
see Listing Archived Object
Versions, linked below.

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:19.750
You disable object
versioning, which

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:22.120
stops future object archiving.

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:24.520
Existing archived
versions of objects

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:26.440
remain in Cloud Storage.

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:29.410
And even though object
versioning is disabled,

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:32.410
both cat versions remain
stored in your bucket

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.110
until you delete
them, either manually

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:38.860
using the command at
gsutil rm or through using

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:42.280
Object Lifecycle Management,
which is a topic for a totally

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:43.780
different video.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.060
Even with object
versioning disabled,

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.910
you can restore one of the
existing archived versions

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:50.740
by making a copy of it.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:54.400
To do so simply named
the copy you make cat.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.100
Once you do this, your
bucket has three versions

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.980
of cat, the two archived
versions and the one

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:02.770
live version that came
from making a copy.

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:03.850
And there you go.

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:07.600
You can now manage versioning
on your own Cloud buckets.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:11.290
On a side note, do keep in mind
that enabling object versioning

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.700
increases storage costs.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:16.990
This can be partially reduced
by configuring Object Lifecycle

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:19.580
Management to delete
older object versions,

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:22.240
but that's for another episode.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:25.300
If you'd like to know more about
managing object versioning,

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:27.310
check out the links
in the description.

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:29.810
Don't forget to
subscribe, give us a Like,

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:32.080
and tell us what you want
to learn about Google Cloud

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:33.100
Storage.

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:36.020
Thanks for joining us on this
quick Byte of Cloud Storage.

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:37.120
See you next time.

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:40.470
